8 worksheets have been graded with 7 problems on each one: $8\cdot 7 = 56$. There are 17 worksheets total, and 8 have been graded. $17-8 = 9$ worksheets remain ungraded. These remaining worksheets have 7 problems on each: $9\cdot 7 = 63$. The number of problems remaining is the product of the number of worksheets remaining multiplied by the number of problems on each one. (The product of 9 and something is the same as the product of 63 and the reciprocal of that number.) $\dfrac{63}{7} = 9$. $\dfrac{9}{1} = 9$. $\dfrac{9}{1} = 9$. $\dfrac{9}{1} = 9$. $\dfrac{72}{8} = 9$. \boxed{63} 
If the answer you provided is $\boxed{63}$, you might be wondering why I wrote all of the extra steps. Most of the work you do in mathematics, like in engineering or physics involves solving equations with known constants.  Math Without Numbers, brought to you by Blue Blazers Engineering, is a side of math where people often don't have calculators, and each calculation is essentially independent or irrelevant within the rest of the calculation. Someone just happened to look at the extra steps I wrote and know they are talking to the people who prefer that 1 additional calculation out of the answer.
